{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Inheritance in Java "}, {"type": "position", "content": 11}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when talking about inheritance in java."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Talk about principles, concepts, and characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob11re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Used To and Would - Negative Forms"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <strong>used to</strong> and <strong>would</strong> to talk about past habits you don't have anymore."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Remember that there are two kinds of verbs:</span> <strong>state verbs</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>and</span> <strong>actions verbs.</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>The state verbs are the ones that don't describe an action,  which means they are static. On the other hand, action verbs describe an action that lasts for a certain period of time.<br><br> That being said, there is a difference in use between</span> <strong>used to</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>and</span> <strong>would</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>to describe past habits. You can't use <strong>would</strong> to describe past events with state verbs, for example:<br><br></span> <strong>Used to:</strong><br><br> <i>When I was little, I used to live in Miami.</i> <strong>Correct!<br><br> Would: </strong><br><br> <i>When I was little, I would live in Miami.</i> <strong>Incorrect!</strong><br><br> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Now, let's study the negative structure of used to. You will need the auxiliary did + not and the main verb in present form, like this:<br><br></span> <strong>Subject + did + not (didn't) + verb in present + complement</strong><br><br> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Example:<br><br><i>I</span> <strong>didn't use to</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>know about inheritance in Java, but now I do.</i><br><br> And now, let's see the negative form of would. You will need the modal would + not and the main verb in present form, like this:<br><br></span> <strong>Subject + would + not + verb in present + complement</strong><br><br> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Example:<br><br><i> When I was a kid, I</span> <strong>wouldn't go</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>to the library to look information about object-oriented programming.</i>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "In my Job, we didn't use to work with Java."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You didn't use to use examples to understand the theory."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Adverbs of Manner"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<strong>Adverbs of manner</strong> are the ones that indicate specific information of the verbs. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Adverbs of manner</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>tell us the \u201chow\u201d of the actions. Usually, these adverbs are placed either after the verb or after the object in the sentence and they end with</span> <strong>\u2013ly.</strong><br><br> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>For example:<br><i> Inheritance in Java means to use and re-use objects, methods, and variables</i><strong> correctly.</strong><br><br> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Remember that there are some adverbs that don't have the termination</span> <strong>\u2013ly.</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>For example: well, hard, fast, etc.<br><br> Let's take a look at the next chart to see more</span> <strong>adverbs of manner:"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I have to work with Java repeatedly this semester. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Those concepts are easily misinterpreted."}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":10,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Adverb of manner\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"\\t\\tMeaning\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"accidentally\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"when something happens by accident\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"badly\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"when something is very bad\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"carefully\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"when you do something and you are very careful or cautious\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"easily\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"when something is very easy\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"hurriedly\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"when something happens very fast\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"neatly\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"when something is very clean;  in order \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"punctually\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"very punctual \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"quietly\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"when something is very silent\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"repeatedly\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"when there is a lot of repetition\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "harness "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to use something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Can you tell me how to  harness inheritance in Java?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob11aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "item "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a piece of something that belongs to a whole "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The main class that provides these items is called parent class."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob11aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "redundant "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that is repeated unnecessarily  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: One class to be accessible by another one without generating redundant codes.  "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob11aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "vice versa "}, {"type": "definition", "content": " the other way around of something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The union employee class is capable of using variables and methods from the parent class and creates its own but not vice versa."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob11aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "Override"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to modify the regular functioning of something "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Definitely! With a child class, you can override those methods."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob11aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": " revamp "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to put something up to date "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You just need to use the original method and revamp it with what you need."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le02ob11aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for information about inheritance usages in java. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write notes and describe in a chart how to use it step by step."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Describe in your own words and with some real examples how it works with methods and variables. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share with your partner and check extra questions."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for information about inheritance usages in java. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I describe in a chart how to use it step by step. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I talk about inheritance in Java and its usages. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob11re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Jack:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Jena, I really need your help. In my Job, we didn't use to work with Java, but now my boss wants me to work on something called Java inheritance. You know that when we were in college I wouldn't pay much attention in our Java class. </span><br>\n<strong>Jena:</strong> <span class=\u201ditalic\u201d> Yes, and I remember you wouldn't go to that class very often. Don't worry, I have to work with Java repeatedly this semester, and I can explain everything to you. </span><br>\n<strong>Jack:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>Thanks, can you tell me how to harness inheritance in Java? I know it has to do with object oriented programming. </span><br><br>"}, {"time": "35000", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob11re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Jena:</strong> <span class=\u201ditalic\u201d> Simple! Inherit in Java is when the program allows methods and variables that belong to one class to be accessible by another one without generating redundant codes. The main class that provides these items is called parent class, also known as superclass. The class that accesses these items is called child class or subclass. </span><br>\n<strong>Jack:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>That's why I wouldn't understand Java in college. Those concepts are easily misinterpreted. </span><br>\n<strong>Jena:</strong> <span class=\u201ditalic\u201d>Well, you didn't use to use examples to understand the theory, let me give you some. Imagine you create a parent class that represents an employee and a subclass that represents specifically a union employee;  the union employee class is capable of using variables and methods from the parent class and creates its own, but not vice versa.  </span><br><br>"}, {"time": "91000", "image": "enpe7n5le02ob11re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Jack:</strong> <span class =\u201ditalic\u201d>It's clear now. One last question. Are child classes able to modify the methods they inherit from the parent classes? </span><br>\n<strong>Jena:</strong> <span class=\u201ditalic\u201d> Definitely! With a child class, you can override those methods. To override a data type, you just need to use the original method and revamp it with what you need. The only requirement is to use the super keyword in front of each method so Java knows you're overriding."}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le02ob11re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n5le02ob11re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"_data": [], "contents": [{"value": "02 (2).jpg", "type": "img"}], "type": "static", "template_override": 1, "id": 2, "title": ""}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about what inheritance means in Java and its relation to object oriented programming."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can provide examples related to inheritance in Java."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can override in regards to inheritance in Java."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What do you know about inheritance? What does it have to do with Java? Why? "}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": " What does inheritance in Java have to do with object-oriented programming?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "What does inheritance in Java mean?"}], "id": 0, "type": "WLC", "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "img_path": ""}, {"stimuli_ids": [1], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " Jack needs a review on inheritance in Java! "}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Content"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "co